    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on some walnut cabinet joinery right now and I'm running into a frustrating issue. Every time I try to cut the dovetails, the wood fibers are splintering at the tips of the pins, even though I'm being careful with the layout. It's compromising the fit and making the joints look messy. Can you give me some advice on how to manage the grain direction better or what I should check with my chisel sharpness?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When working with dovetail joints, it's essential to consider the wood fibers' direction to maintain a smooth cut and prevent splintering. To manage grain direction, try marking the pin and tail cutlines with a reference line, and ensure the reference line is parallel to the long grain direction. Additionally, sharp chisels will also play a significant role, as dull chisel edges can exacerbate the problem and cause shattering. Sharpening the chisel and using the correct chisel angle can also help minimize splintering.",
      "source": "target",
