    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the tip. i've been sharpening them on my whetstones regularly but maybe i'm missing something. i noticed the splintering happens more often when i'm working on the end grain sections. is there a specific way i should be positioning the chisel or maybe a different way to approach the relief cuts to keep the fibers from blowing out?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Working with end grain sections can be particularly challenging, as the fibers tend to be more brittle in this direction. To reduce splintering, it's essential to create a more gradual relief cut in the end grain sections, rather than applying too much downward pressure with the chisel. Try using a shallow, light touch to guide the chisel, and focus on keeping the blade parallel to the table. This will help the fibers spring back as you remove the material, reducing the likelihood of them splitting.",
      "source": "target",
